-- title:
--   Minimal polynomial of λ(q^q) has coefficients in ℚ[λ]
-- statement:
--   Work inside the Laurent series field $\mathbb Q((\mathfrak q))$, and let $\lambda :=$ `lambdaModC ℚ` be the coefficientwise image under $\mathbb Z \to \mathbb Q$ of the explicit integral Laurent series `lambdaInt`, namely $\mathfrak q$ times the eighth power of the eta product, times the series obtained from the sixteenth power of that eta product by multiplying all exponents by $4$, times the series obtained from `dedekindEtaUnitInv` by multiplying all exponents by $2$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \neq 2$, and let `lambdaNModC ℚ q` be the series obtained from $\lambda$ by multiplying all exponents by $q$, i.e. the substitution $\mathfrak q \mapsto \mathfrak q^{q}$ applied to $\lambda$. Consider the minimal polynomial of this series over the intermediate field $\mathbb Q(\lambda)$ of $\mathbb Q((\mathfrak q))$ generated by $\lambda$. The assertion is that for every natural number $k$, the $k$-th coefficient of that minimal polynomial, regarded as an element of $\mathbb Q((\mathfrak q))$, lies in the $\mathbb Q$-subalgebra of $\mathbb Q((\mathfrak q))$ generated by $\lambda$; since $\lambda$ is transcendental over $\mathbb Q$, this subalgebra is the polynomial ring $\mathbb Q[\lambda]$.
--
--   This is the integrality statement underlying the modular equation at level two: the minimal polynomial of $\lambda(\mathfrak q^q)$ over $\mathbb Q(\lambda)$, of degree $q+1$, has polynomial rather than merely rational coefficients in $\lambda$. It is used to produce the modular polynomial data attached to $\lambda$ in [`ModularCurve.nonempty_lambdaModularPolynomialData`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_lambdaModularPolynomialData).
